WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.880
This is an Ogham stone,

00:00:01.880 --> 00:00:04.580
or "ogg-em" stone, depending on
whose pronunciation you follow.

00:00:04.589 --> 00:00:08.320
The carvings on here are from an alphabet
unlike anything else in the world,

00:00:08.320 --> 00:00:11.820
an alphabet that is literally
an exception to modern rules.

00:00:11.820 --> 00:00:14.860
I’m not talking about the Roman letters
on the face:

00:00:14.860 --> 00:00:17.600
I’m talking about the markings
carved into the corner.

00:00:17.600 --> 00:00:20.940
Those markings are in Ogham -- it’s a way
of writing down the early Irish language

00:00:20.940 --> 00:00:22.670
with marks like these.

00:00:22.670 --> 00:00:24.920
There are only about 400 surviving stones
like this,

00:00:24.920 --> 00:00:27.420
found in Ireland and
the western parts of the UK.

00:00:27.420 --> 00:00:30.820
This particular one is about 1500 years old,

00:00:30.820 --> 00:00:33.060
and it comes from Devon
in the south west of England.

00:00:33.060 --> 00:00:35.080
It’s on display here at the British Museum.

00:00:35.080 --> 00:00:38.440
The inscription is a name. Ogham stones
are mostly used to record names,

00:00:38.440 --> 00:00:41.850
either as a tombstone or
as a marker of land ownership.

00:00:41.850 --> 00:00:44.800
You read this along the stemline,
along the corner.

00:00:44.800 --> 00:00:47.940
Each character is made of one to five markings,
which will all be on

00:00:47.940 --> 00:00:52.179
one side of the line, the other side of the line,
through the line, or on the line.

00:00:52.179 --> 00:00:56.159
When modern scholars started to analyse this
script, they wanted to write it down on paper,

00:00:56.159 --> 00:00:58.780
and they adapted it a little
to make it easier for them:

00:00:58.780 --> 00:01:01.660
they changed it so it always went left to right,

00:01:01.660 --> 00:01:04.690
and each phrase was drawn on
a horizontal line so you could easily tell

00:01:04.690 --> 00:01:06.740
which marks were on which side.

00:01:06.740 --> 00:01:10.980
Some Ogham inscriptions, on later stones or
on other artefacts, do actually carve their

00:01:10.980 --> 00:01:16.800
own stemline into a flat surface, so adding
that line in print wasn't too much of a stretch.

00:01:16.800 --> 00:01:19.820
And after all, trying to fold a bit of paper
and sketch markings on the corner

00:01:19.820 --> 00:01:22.760
wouldn't be easy to work with
for academic papers.

00:01:22.760 --> 00:01:26.640
And that left us, years later,
with an interesting technology problem.

00:01:26.640 --> 00:01:31.240
When it came time to encode Ogham characters
as 1s and 0s, to fold them into Unicode,

00:01:31.240 --> 00:01:34.626
the international standard for
how to display text on a computer:

00:01:34.626 --> 00:01:40.000
Ogham became the only language in Unicode
where a space is not a space.

00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:43.760
A space character, to a computer, has three
properties: it has a certain width,

00:01:43.760 --> 00:01:46.443
you don't display or print anything in that width, and

00:01:46.443 --> 00:01:48.580
if your text has run out of room on a line,

00:01:48.580 --> 00:01:52.300
you can go back to the previous space character
and replace it with a line break.

00:01:52.300 --> 00:01:55.900
Now, there have been well-understood variations
on those for years.

00:01:55.900 --> 00:01:58.590
Two of those rules are flouted
all the time.

00:01:58.590 --> 00:02:03.120
You can have a non-breaking space, like the one
between a word and a French quotation mark.

00:02:03.120 --> 00:02:08.060
That space has width, it has nothing displayed
in it, but you can't put a line break there.

00:02:08.060 --> 00:02:11.019
The word and punctuation must travel together.

00:02:11.020 --> 00:02:14.440
You can also have a zero-width space, which
sounds like a ridiculous idea,

00:02:14.440 --> 00:02:17.648
but it's a good way to tell a computer that,
if there isn't room,

00:02:17.648 --> 00:02:21.799
it's OK to break a long string of characters
somewhere that it otherwise wouldn't.

00:02:21.799 --> 00:02:23.819
These are all commonly used.

00:02:23.819 --> 00:02:28.269
But until Ogham was added to Unicode, the
rule that a space character must be empty

00:02:28.269 --> 00:02:31.269
had never been broken.
Why would it? It's a space.

00:02:31.269 --> 00:02:34.489
Well, an Ogham space includes that stemline.

00:02:34.489 --> 00:02:38.299
The line doesn’t stop between words, because
the corner doesn’t stop between words.

00:02:38.299 --> 00:02:41.459
The space is not a space...
but it behaves like one.

00:02:41.459 --> 00:02:43.459
It can be replaced with a line break.

00:02:43.459 --> 00:02:46.020
If you spread an Ogham inscription
over two lines,

00:02:46.020 --> 00:02:49.189
the space character vanishes,
same as in English.

00:02:49.189 --> 00:02:52.460
Now, Ogham isn't the only language that uses
a separator like this.

00:02:52.460 --> 00:02:55.620
Ancient Latin used an interpunct,
a middle dot, the same way.

00:02:55.620 --> 00:03:00.269
But in modern usage that is not a space,
and modern usage wins.

00:03:00.269 --> 00:03:02.820
Ogham is the only case where
modern folks have gone,

00:03:02.820 --> 00:03:05.620
yeah, okay, it's a space that also
involves drawing something.

00:03:05.620 --> 00:03:07.530
It's a space that isn't a space.

00:03:07.530 --> 00:03:08.920
There's been an actual argument about it,

00:03:08.920 --> 00:03:11.880
down in one of the mailing lists for
linguists and computer science nerds

00:03:11.880 --> 00:03:13.320
at the Unicode Consortium.

00:03:13.329 --> 00:03:16.090
The Irish contingent had some
very strong opinions.

00:03:16.090 --> 00:03:18.680
And the final ruling: yep.

00:03:18.680 --> 00:03:20.720
It's a space that's also a line.

00:03:20.730 --> 00:03:23.940
This is one of the things I love about linguistics:
an ancient script,

00:03:23.940 --> 00:03:26.260
carved into stones more than
a millennium ago,

00:03:26.260 --> 00:03:30.189
is an exception to a rule that
I never even realised was there.

00:03:30.189 --> 00:03:32.600
A space doesn’t have to be a space.

